garden.disclaimer="Garden care by the moon" is a traditional practice. The days shown follow the biodynamic tradition and are based on the Moon’s position before the zodiac constellations (root, leaf, flower and fruit days).

garden.part.root=Root days
garden.part.leaf=Leaf days
garden.part.flower=Flower days
garden.part.fruit=Fruit days
garden.biodynamic.ascending=Ascending moon — tradition favours sowing, grafting and harvesting above-ground crops.
garden.biodynamic.descending=Descending moon — tradition favours transplanting, root work and composting.
garden.biodynamic.root.guidance=A traditional root day: associated with root crops such as carrots, beets, onions and potatoes.
garden.biodynamic.leaf.guidance=A traditional leaf day: associated with leafy crops such as lettuce, spinach and cabbage.
garden.biodynamic.flower.guidance=A traditional flower day: associated with flowering plants and herbs harvested for bloom.
garden.biodynamic.fruit.guidance=A traditional fruit and seed day: associated with fruiting and seed crops such as tomatoes, beans and grains.
garden.biodynamic.buffer.perigee=Note: the Moon reaches perigee (closest) in this period — traditionally a rest period.
garden.biodynamic.buffer.apogee=Note: the Moon reaches apogee (farthest) in this period.
garden.biodynamic.badday.title=Unfavourable garden day (Moon at node)
garden.biodynamic.badday.pdf=Rest day
garden.biodynamic.badday.description=Unfavourable garden day (Moon at node) — rest rather than sow.

garden.pdf.legend.root=Root days: the Moon stands before an earth constellation. In the folk tradition these days suit root crops such as carrots, beetroot, radishes and onions — for sowing, cultivating and harvesting whatever grows below the ground.
garden.pdf.legend.leaf=Leaf days: the Moon stands before a water constellation. In the folk tradition these days suit leafy crops such as lettuce, spinach, chard, cabbage and kale — for sowing and tending plants grown for their foliage.
garden.pdf.legend.flower=Flower days: the Moon stands before an air constellation. In the folk tradition these days suit flowers and flowering plants such as broccoli, ornamental blooms and herbs gathered in flower — for sowing and tending what blossoms.
garden.pdf.legend.fruit=Fruit days: the Moon stands before a fire constellation. In the folk tradition these days suit fruiting and seed crops such as tomatoes, beans, peppers, squash and grains — for sowing and harvesting fruit or seed.
garden.pdf.legend.badday=Unfavourable garden day: the Moon crosses a node, or an eclipse falls in the period. In the folk tradition a day to rest — neither sow nor plant.
